  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe blocked/stable synoptic pattern over the Great Lakes has begun \nto break down early this morning. Prior ridging continues to flatten \nand fold into northeastern CONUS, trailing the early week coastal \nsystem's departure. Meanwhile, much longer wavelength disturbances \nupstream are tracking zonally across central Canada and southern \nCONUS. Southeast Michigan will be positioned between these two waves \nthrough the rest of the week, eventually leading to periods of \nrainfall. At present, leading geopotential height falls have \noverspread Lower Michigan, marked by a thickening canopy of cirrus \naloft. The southern stream wave/closed-low absorbed what remains of \neastern Pacific Hurricane Polo leading to the advection of an \nequatorial airmass from the Four Corners region up to James Bay.\n\nThe distribution of saturation is currently skewed toward the upper \nlevels as top-down moistening ramps up over the next 12 hours. Given \nthat the column has been abnormally dry since last Thursday (see \nKDTX RAOBs since 24.12Z), it will take time to prime the low levels \nand achieve adequate saturation depths before rainfall becomes more \nprevalent. Latest mosaic radar imagery shows a sharp cutoff to the \nrain edge, currently confined to areas west of a Chicago to Gaylord \nline. The swath of rainfall should migrate eastward throughout the \nday as the theta-e ridge rolls southeast by this afternoon as \nembedded shortwave energy gets deflected away from the higher \namplitude northern stream wave. The majority of 00Z/06Z solutions \ndepict some degree of measurable precipitation for most areas by 00Z \nthis evening. The lowest confidence in this initial round of diurnal \nshowers continues for locations along/south of I-94 where saturation \nprocesses are the most delayed.\n\nThe axis of persistent tropical moisture advection drifts over Lower \nMichigan Thursday amidst a shifting deformation zone and reinforcing \nCVA.  This unfolds as the southern stream low starts to open/phase \nwith the deeper continental wave to the north. Expect longer and \nmore continuous periods of rainfall as Thursday progresses with \nnotably muggier conditions. Surface dewpoints easily climb into the \nmid 60s, if not upper 60s, while 850 mb temperatures rise into the \nupper teens (Celsius). PWATs respond accordingly, climbing above \n1.75 inches for some areas, which raises the prospect for localized \nheavy rainfall (see Hydrology Discussion). From a stability \nperspective, the thunder threat appears quite low given the warm \nadvection scheme, before an approaching cold front extending from \nthe Ontario surface reflection slowly tracks through the region.\n\nAggressive post-frontal system-relative isentropic downglide leads \nto a quick conclusion to showers, before the midday hours Friday. N \nnorthwest flow arrives downstream of 1027 mb high pressure over the \nUpper Midwest. Seasonably cool and dry though the weekend.",
